{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And Elohim saw the light, that it was good.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the word וירא denotes the thing's establishment and persistence by the will of the Holy One, blessed be He — from the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory, 'I see the words of Edomo.' And so you will find in every single day of the work of creation. The intent is that He decreed upon each of the creatures among all existent things their persistence and establishment, that their existence would be by His will; and if His will were separated from them for a moment, they would become naught. And therefore He sealed the work of creation with ״והנה טוב מאד״ — for He desired their existence. And the reason He added את האור and did not say וירא אלהים כי טוב without it, is that had He said so, it would have referred back to the creation of the heavens and the earth, and He had not yet decreed their establishment, for they had not stood in their power — since on the second day the firmament was made, and on the third day the waters and the dust were separated and the dry land was made and called earth; and only then did He decree their establishment and say וירא אלהים כי טוב." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: וירא — what good innovation did He see in it? He saw that it was not fitting for the wicked to use it in this world, so He arose and stored it away for the righteous in the world to come." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: את האור comes to include the matter that R. Akiva attained, and all kinds of its luminaries that exist from the truth of this light. And concerning that matter the sages testified of him that he entered in peace and emerged in peace. And it is further possible that it comes to include the souls that were created with the light, and thus it is alluded to in Job (Job 38): ״איזה הדרך ישכן אור״, and it is written (Job 38): ״ידעת כי אז תולד ומספר ימיך רבים״, as the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained in his commentary on Job; and thus it is written (1 Chronicles 4:23): ״עם המלך במלאכתו ישבו שם״." "<b>And Elohim separated between the light and between the darkness.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this darkness is the absence of light, and it is not the supernal darkness that He mentioned in ״וחשך על פני תהום״, for that darkness is fire; and He did not mention כי טוב on the second day. And on the third day, when the work of the waters was completed, He mentioned טוב twice — once for the completion of the work of the waters and once for the day itself. And I ask about this: what difference does it make that the work of the waters was not completed? Since the work of the firmament and the creation of the angels, which were created on the second day, were completed, it would have been fitting to say כי טוב upon the completion of that work and creation in the firmament and in the angels, and He should have decreed their establishment and said of them כי טוב, for they are no less than all the rest of the work of creation of which כי טוב was said. And I would say that from this there is a mighty proof that the lower world, at the time Israel do the will of the Place, is the core of existence: for even though on the second day the creation of the firmament and the angels — which are the upper world — was completed, there was no perfection until the work of the waters was completed on the third day with the utterance יקוו, and the lower world was made, for the entire world was water and they needed to be gathered into one place so that the dry land would appear. And when the utterance יקוו came, the upper world was established through the creation of this lower world, and with this existence was complete and fitting to mention in it כי טוב. And the general meaning of this statement, which our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded — ״because the work of the waters was not completed״ — is that because the work of the waters in the lower world was not completed, He did not wish to mention כי טוב concerning the creation of the upper world; if so, this lower world is the core of existence. And perhaps for this reason the dry land is called ארץ, because in it the will and intention of the Holy One, blessed be He, in existence was completed — from the language of the verse (Malachi 3): ״כי תהיו אתם ארץ חפץ״; and He attributed this naming to the Holy One, blessed be He, as it says ״ויקרא אלהים ליבשה ארץ״. Also on the fourth day, in the utterance ״יהי מאורות״, the verse explains that the luminaries were created only for the use of this world, as it says ״להאיר על הארץ״ — and behold, this demonstrates that the supernal beings, despite being great and mighty, were created for the sake of the lower beings. And I have a further proof from the words of Moses our master, peace be upon him, in the verse (Deuteronomy 10): ״הן ליהוה אלהיך השמים ושמי השמים״ — that this lower world is the core of existence, as I shall write there, with the help of the Holy One, blessed be He. And all this is by way of the Midrash of our Rabbis of blessed memory: ״גדולים צדיקים יותר ממלאכי השרת״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> Why was כי טוב not said on the second day? Because on it Gehenna was created, as it is said (Isaiah 30:33): ״כי ערוך מאתמול תפתה״ — a day that has an אתמול (yesterday) but has no שלשום (day after tomorrow). And further, they expounded, of blessed memory: because on it dissension was created, as it says ״ויהי מבדיל בין מים למים״. R. Taviomi said: If dissension that is for the repair of the world and its settlement does not have כי טוב written in it, then dissension that is for the world's confusion — all the more so! And the explanation of the matter is that the second day is the beginning of change (שנוי), and therefore it is called שני, and it is the cause of all separation and change. What they said, that dissension and Gehenna were created on that day — it is to reflect upon this, that everyone who stirs up dissension is judged in Gehenna. And since dissension and Gehenna were created on that day, we learn that it is a harmful day; and therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory forbade starting any work on it, and they said: one does not begin on the second day. And therefore they also forbade eating pairs, for it is a harmful thing; and this is what they said: a person should not eat two and should not drink two. And this dissension that was on the second day was the beginning of all dissension and its cause. And therefore כי טוב was not said of it, for it is the cause of the causes after it; and from it onward there were those who deviated from and altered the will of HaShem, blessed be He — for on the third day He said ״תוצא הארץ עץ פרי״ and it did not do so, but rather ״עץ עושה פרי״; and on the fourth day the moon complained and said: it is impossible for two kings to use one crown; and on the fifth day the mate of Leviathan was salted, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: He castrated the male and killed the female and salted her for the righteous in the world to come; and on the sixth day Adam and Eve sinned and were banished from the Garden of Eden. If so, we find in all the days after the second day matters of accusation and alteration of intention and separation of will and division of opinion among the creatures, and their cause is the second day, for all of them draw their power from it and follow after it."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {3 "Printed citation reads (ד\"ה א ד כ״ג); the verse quoted, עם המלך במלאכתו ישבו שם, is 1 Chronicles 4:23. The abbreviation ד״ה resolved as דברי הימים א (1 Chronicles).", 4 "Source citation reads (ישעיה ל ל״ג) for Isaiah 30:33 — appears to be a typographical error for ל״ג (33); normalized to Isaiah 30:33."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:24:51.678518048Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}